# PEM MCP

This workspace runs Prompt Engineering Matrix as a Cursor MCP server (`pem`). Any agent in this project should use those tools. Do not `PYTHONPATH=... python -c "from prompt_matrix.pipelines import run_workflow"`.

## Tools

- `pem_compile` — dialect prompt only. No model call.
- `pem_combine` — two or more models draft, then merge. Default primary `gemini`, extra `deepseek`.
- `pem_critique_rewrite` — attempt → persona critique → final. Personas: `redhat`, `security`, `tokens`, `schema`, `code`.
- `pem_dialect_lint` — static dialect check. No model call.
- `pem_export` — saved class to `cursorrules` / `mdc` / `fabric` / `dspy`.

Intents are `research`, `design`, `comparison`, `debug`, `analysis`. There is no `code` intent. PEM is domain-agnostic: the case is the uploaded file and the user's task, not a built-in product.

Keys stay in `prompt_matrix/.env`. Never print them. Cursor has no PEM chat API; `target_ai: cursor` is compile-only.

If the `pem` MCP namespace is missing from this session, tell the user to reload MCP (Cursor Settings → MCP) or start a new chat. Do not invent a workaround that pastes API keys into the shell.
